resolved_issues.body:
### Version Number

7.54.1

### Codesandbox/Expo snack

https://codesandbox.io/p/sandbox/ecstatic-wind-cd8j4f

### Steps to reproduce

I reopened #12479 
You closed this issue saying "This is expected behaviour"
I don't agree with you. For me it doesn't look like expected behaviour. 
My assumtion is that you closed this issue because it is quite rare case and you have other priorities right know. 
If it is true. That is okey. I am willing to devote my time to investigate this bug. It is not much of a problem to me because your library is really helpfull and I am glad to make it event more better. 
I created previous issue without much details because I thought you could easily find the reason for this bug as the maintainer.

This time I simplified example even more. I left only two inputs. Each input resides in separate component. There is useWatch inside each component and input value is updated using setValue. If I used Controller to control the input then the bug wouldn't reproduce. This bug occurs only when updating values using setValue. 
And also one important detail is that title and description is an array of strings with one element. If title and description were just string, then the bug wouldn't reproduce. I'll soon explain why is that.
Green border flash in the video below indicates that component rerendered.

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9880e97e-84c9-45c7-a9ed-cae470081433

First question why removing DevTool fixes this bug.
What is inside DevTool causes this bug?

By reducing DevTool part by part I found out that without `proxyToObject(formState)` the bug doesn't occur

So here is the minimal version of DevTool that causes the bug
```typescript
const DevTool = () => {
   const formState = useFormState({control})
   
   proxyToObject(formState);
}
```

What is `proxyToObject`

```typescript
export function proxyToObject<T extends Record<string, any>>(proxy: T) {
  return Reflect.ownKeys(proxy).reduce((prev, key) => {
    prev[key as keyof T] = proxy[key as keyof T];
    return prev;
  }, {} as T);
}
```

It just utility that remove proxy wrapper from the object.

What is `formState`?

it is result of useFormState.
What is result of useFormState?

```typescript
function useFormState<TFieldValues extends FieldValues = FieldValues>(
  props?: UseFormStateProps<TFieldValues>,
): UseFormStateReturn<TFieldValues> {
  const methods = useFormContext<TFieldValues>();
  const { control = methods.control, disabled, name, exact } = props || {};
  const [formState, updateFormState] = React.useState(control._formState);

  const _localProxyFormState = React.useRef({
    isDirty: false,
    isLoading: false,
    dirtyFields: false,
    touchedFields: false,
    validatingFields: false,
    isValidating: false,
    isValid: false,
    errors: false,
  });

  // ...
  
  return getProxyFormState(
    formState,
    control,
    _localProxyFormState.current,
    false,
  );
}

```

result of `useFormState` is result of `getProxyFormState`.

What is result `getProxyFormState`?

```typescript
export default <TFieldValues extends FieldValues, TContext = any>(
  formState: FormState<TFieldValues>,
  control: Control<TFieldValues, TContext>,
  localProxyFormState?: ReadFormState,
  isRoot = true,
) => {
  const result = {
    defaultValues: control._defaultValues,
  } as typeof formState;

  for (const key in formState) {
    Object.defineProperty(result, key, {
      get: () => {
        const _key = key as keyof FormState<TFieldValues> & keyof ReadFormState;

        if (control._proxyFormState[_key] !== VALIDATION_MODE.all) {
          control._proxyFormState[_key] = !isRoot || VALIDATION_MODE.all;
        }

        localProxyFormState && (localProxyFormState[_key] = true);
        return formState[_key];
      },
    });
  }

  return result;
};
```

It is control._formState wrapped in proxy. 
This proxy is partially responsibe for the bug. I will explain it a little bit later.

Now when we understand what formState and what is proxyToObject?
Why this code causes the bug?
```typescript
proxyToObject(formState)
```
I don't know yet.
I know that converting formState from proxy to object we modify `control._proxyFormState`.
It might be the reason.

```
control._proxyFormState[_key] = !isRoot || VALIDATION_MODE.all;
// isRoot false, so control._proxyFormState[_key] = true;
```
What is inside control._proxyFormState in the end?

This object
```typescript
{
  isDirty: true,
  isValidating:  true,
  isLoading:  true,
  isSubmitted:  true,
  isSubmitting:  true,
  isSubmitSuccessful:  true,
  isValid:  true,
  submitCount: true,
  dirtyFields:  true,
  touchedFields:  true,
  validatingFields:  true,
  errors: true,
  disabled:  true,
  defaultValues: true,
}
```

Why before resetting the form this bug doesn't occur?
What happens inside `reset` method that causes this bug?
From first look I couldn't tell.

Why after resetting the form setValue triggers rerender of all components?
What happens inside setValue that triggers rerender of all components instead of specific ones?


```typescript
 _subjects.values.next({
   name: _state.mount ? name : undefined,
   values: { ..._formValues },
 });
```

For some reason _state.mount is false after resetting the form?
But why is that?
What are the places where _state.mount can be changed to false?
One of the places is `reset` method.

```typescript
_state.mount =
  !_proxyFormState.isValid ||
  !!keepStateOptions.keepIsValid ||
  !!keepStateOptions.keepDirtyValues;
``` 

if _proxyFormState.isValid equals true, then _state.mount will equal false.
what is _proxyFormState?
_proxyFormState is control._proxyFormState that was modified earlier in the render of DevTool component when unwraping formState object from proxy.

as we remember _proxyFormState.isValid was true after running proxyToObject(formState).

What does _proxyFormState.isValid means?
It means that formState's isValid property was read somewhere. 
But I don't understand how state._mount is connected to reading of formState's isValid property.
What does state._mount variable mean?

let's come back to `setValue`

```typescript
 _subjects.values.next({
   name: _state.mount ? name : undefined,
   values: { ..._formValues },
 });
```
Why do all components that have useWatch rerender?
Is it connected to _state.mount being false?
if _state.mount is false, then `name` is undefined. 

What does _subject.values.next do?

it calls all _subject.values observers

```typescript
export default <T>(): Subject<T> => {
  const next = (value: T) => {
    for (const observer of _observers) {
      observer.next && observer.next(value);
    }
  };
  // ..
};
```

What are the observers? Where do they come from?

Observers can come from useWatch hook. I have 2 of them in my example.
One in `Title` component other in `Description` component

```typescript
export function useWatch<TFieldValues extends FieldValues>(
  props?: UseWatchProps<TFieldValues>,
) {
  const methods = useFormContext();
  const {
    control = methods.control,
    name,
    defaultValue,
    disabled,
    exact,
  } = props || {};
  const _name = React.useRef(name);

  _name.current = name;

  useSubscribe({
    disabled,
    subject: control._subjects.values,
    next: (formState: { name?: InternalFieldName; values?: FieldValues }) => {
      if (
        shouldSubscribeByName(
          _name.current as InternalFieldName,
          formState.name,
          exact,
        )
      ) {
        updateValue(
          cloneObject(
            generateWatchOutput(
              _name.current as InternalFieldName | InternalFieldName[],
              control._names,
              formState.values || control._formValues,
              false,
              defaultValue,
            ),
          ),
        );
      }
    },
  });

  const [value, updateValue] = React.useState(
    control._getWatch(
      name as InternalFieldName,
      defaultValue as DeepPartialSkipArrayKey<TFieldValues>,
    ),
  );

  React.useEffect(() => control._removeUnmounted());

  return value;
}

```

What does `observer.next` do?
It calls `next` callback.
What is `next` callback?

It is a function that calls updateValue if shouldSubscribeByName return true?

```typescript
     if (
        shouldSubscribeByName(
          _name.current as InternalFieldName,
          formState.name,
          exact,
        )
      ) {
        updateValue(
          cloneObject(
            generateWatchOutput(
              _name.current as InternalFieldName | InternalFieldName[],
              control._names,
              formState.values || control._formValues,
              false,
              defaultValue,
            ),
          ),
        );
      }

```

What is inside `shouldSubscribeByName`?

```typescript
export default <T extends string | string[] | undefined>(
  name?: T,
  signalName?: string,
  exact?: boolean,
) =>
  !name ||
  !signalName ||
  name === signalName ||
  convertToArrayPayload(name).some(
    (currentName) =>
      currentName &&
      (exact
        ? currentName === signalName
        : currentName.startsWith(signalName) ||
          signalName.startsWith(currentName)),
  );

```

If all our components rerendered, that it means that shouldSubscribeByName returned true.
Why is that?
As we can see shouldSubscribeByName returns true if name is undefined.
As we remember name is undefined because state._mount is false.
```typescript
 _subjects.values.next({
   name: _state.mount ? name : undefined,
   values: { ..._formValues },
 });
```
I don't understand why name is undefined if _state.mount is false? What is the purpose of this?

So now we understand why all components that have useWatch rerender.
why do only components that watch for complex values (objects, arrays) rerender?
why don't components that watch for primitive values rerender
because of react optimizations
react is smart enough to not rereder if value didn't change from previous render
in case of complex values this optimization doesn't work
because [] !== []
in case of primitive values it works because 2 === 2

What are the workarounds?

We can set state._mount to true after resetting the form

```
control.reset()
control.state._mount = true;
```

### Expected behaviour

setValue should trigger rerender only of components that watch for the value that was updated 

### What browsers are you seeing the problem on?

Firefox, Chrome, Safari, Edge

### Relevant log output

_No response_

